 ## Overview
-Early Ray Marching Termination (Threshold) is an optimization technique that halts the ray marching loop when the accumulated transmission falls below a user-defined small value, thereby skipping the computation of negligible volume contributions [S1, unofficial]. It is primarily used to accelerate rendering in dense volumes or to implement "picking" interactions in volume visualization systems by identifying the first location where opacity exceeds a threshold [S2]. Unlike standard constant step marching, which samples until exit or hit, this method trades statistical accuracy for performance by discarding the "long tail" of the volume's contribution [S1, unofficial].
+Early Ray Marching Termination (Threshold) is an optimization that halts the ray marching loop when the accumulated transmission falls below a user-defined small value, thereby skipping the computation of negligible volume contributions [S1, unofficial]. It trades statistical accuracy for performance by discarding the "long tail" of the volume's contribution [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard constant step marching, which samples until exit or hit, this method stops early when further contributions are deemed insignificant [S1, unofficial].
 
